WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.235
-Our first guest tonight
is CNN's

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.936
chief Washington correspondent

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:05.838
and the anchor of
"CNN Tonight with Jake Tapper,"

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:09.676
which airs weeknights on CNN
through the midterms.

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.877
His first cover story
for "The Atlantic,"

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:15.081
which I've got right here...
[ Laughter ]

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:17.383
..."This is Not Justice:
The Story of C.J. Rice,"

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:18.985
is out now.
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:21.421
our friend Jake Tapper,
everybody.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.922
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:32.932 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:38.671 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.706
Welcome back.
-Thank you, thank you.

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:43.342
Don't --
Don't ignore the kicks.

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.611
-I like that you took advantage
of the fact that

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:47.713
we're a little more casual
here now to wear your --

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:48.815
Those are Phillies kicks.

00:00:48.815 --> 00:00:50.216
-These are
Philadelphia Phillies kicks.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.383
You may have heard,
they're in the World Series.

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:53.519
-They're in the World Series.
[ Cheers ]

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:56.656
Pretty nice time
to be a Philadelphia sports fan,

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:57.890
I would say, right?
-It's great.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.759
Yeah, the Eagles are 7-0.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:00.960
There's something
going on with soccer.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.762
-Yeah.
And there you go.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:05.798
Now, I want to ask,
because you have moved to --

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:07.099
through the midterms,
you're doing your show

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:08.801
at 9:00 p.m., a prime-time show.
-Yes.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.769
-You used to do your show
at 4:00,, a perfect time

00:01:10.769 --> 00:01:13.472
to wrap up your day's work
and then watch baseball.

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:14.607
This is terrible timing.

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:16.876
-For the World Series,
yes, it's bad.

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:19.946
I mean, I do have the MLB app
on my phone, so...

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.081
-Yeah. I've noticed during
a lot of your interviews,

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:23.616
you're definitely watching
something else.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.152
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:29.122
-If you turn on
and I seem distracted

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:33.092
or I'm like, "Yes!"
then it's not about the economy.

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:34.860
-Right.
I'm like, "Wow!

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.496
He's really psyched
about inflation," yeah.

00:01:37.496 --> 00:01:39.599
-"He seems to be supporting
higher crime numbers?"

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:42.201
-So that's interesting.
So during a commercial break,

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:44.270
you're not asking people
for scores.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.205
You're fully looking down.
-MLB app.

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:48.641
And, in fact, like,
I have found it so much easier

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.411
to be a sports fan now
because of my phone.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:54.013
-Yes.
-Like, the MLB app,

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:56.549
Sunday Ticket -- like,
it makes it much better.

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.618
You get alerts.
-I used to, in college,

00:01:59.618 --> 00:02:03.022
go to a pay phone and call just
to hear what the scores were.

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.491
[ Laughter ]
-That's at least technology.

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:08.260
I used to just wait
for the newspaper to come in...

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.763
[ Laughter ]
...turn to the sports pages.

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.331
"Oh, Schmitty."

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:13.800
-Speaking of Mike Schmidt...
-Yes.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.468
-I know that you --
-Mike Schmidt, the greatest

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:17.169
third baseman of all time,
in case you don't know.

00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:18.537
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah.

00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:21.340
-So, by the way --
so this is modern Mike Schmidt.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.641
-That's me and Schmitty, yes.

00:02:22.641 --> 00:02:25.144
We went to a game in July.
A dream.

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:26.245
-And a true gentleman.

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:27.646
Now here's the thing
I want to ask you.

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:29.649
Mike Schmidt, he famously had
a mustache when he played.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:32.251
-He was a redhead
with a mustache.

00:02:32.251 --> 00:02:33.619
Very iconic look.

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:35.054
He looks very different.
-My dad has a mustache.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:36.589
Still has his mustache.
-Yeah.

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.424
-And a lot of people,
when I was growing up,

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.526
a lot of people told me my dad
looked like Mike Schmidt.

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:42.027
You're a Phillies fan.

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:44.096
I'm gonna show you
three pictures.

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:46.231
You have to tell me,
is it Mike Schmidt,

00:02:46.231 --> 00:02:47.667
or is it my dad?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:50.202
-Now, just so you know,

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:53.039
I actually literally collect
Mike Schmidt baseball cards.

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:54.440
-Right.
-So I have seen all of these

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.208
cards before.
-So you'll have an advantage,

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:58.110
but it's still pretty tricky.
-You think so?

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.079
Okay. Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:01.814
So that -- that looks like
Mike Schmidt.

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:02.849
-Okay.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.216
-That looks like
the actual Mike Schmidt.

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:06.519
-That's the actual Mike Schmidt.
-Okay.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.487
That is definitely
the actual Mike Schmidt.

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:09.889
I have that card.
-There we go.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:10.923
And...

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:13.759
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:15.828
Don't --
Don't answer right away.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:16.962
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.962 --> 00:03:19.932
-That's -- That's --
That's fake Schmidt.

00:03:19.932 --> 00:03:21.233
-That's fake Schmidt.

00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:23.536
And when you see it like this,
you really do think,

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:25.238
"Did he ever
look like Mike Schmidt?"

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:28.774
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.774 --> 00:03:30.043
-That's like Mike Schmidt

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:31.511
if you put Mike Schmidt,
like, in a microwave.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:33.446
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:35.114
-Mike-rowave Schmidt.
-Nice.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.250
-There you go, yeah.
Not really that nice.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.384
-Well, I didn't mean like --

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.685
Your dad's a very handsome man.

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:42.855
-Oh, nice save, Jake Tapper.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:46.125
-I just mean it's off from --
-No. That's always --

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:47.927
There was a slang when
Jake and I were growing up.

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:49.828
You would say,
if a man was really handsome,

00:03:49.828 --> 00:03:51.798
you'd be like, "Ooh, look who
just got out of the microwave."

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.866
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.635
-It's an '80s thing.
-You wouldn't understand.

00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:57.870
-It's when microwaves were new.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.872
-It's why you didn't get
the "Cocoon" joke.

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:03.275
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.275 --> 00:04:06.946
So, your son, Jack, is 13 now.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:09.482
-He's 13, yes.
-So during the pandemic,

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:12.051
I would imagine that was
probably a sweet-spot age

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.820
to go back and watch --
did you watch a lot of TV?

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.088
Did you introduce him
to a lot of stuff?

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:17.757
-I did.
He's a tough customer.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:20.359
We watched "Band of Brothers."
-Perfect show.

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:22.428
-He's really into police,

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.931
and he's really into
World War II victories.

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.866
-Yep. Okay, gotcha.
He doesn't like the losses.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:29.835
-No. I just want to make sure
he's rooting for the right side.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:32.071
-Yeah, yeah, exactly.
-So, yeah, we watched all of

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:35.375
"Band of Brothers," and then we
got really into the show called

00:04:35.375 --> 00:04:38.010
"The Rookie",
which is a Nathan Fillion

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:41.213
police procedural on ABC,
and it's a great show.

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:43.516
It's a lot of fun.
-And then I guess Nathan

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:45.485
must have found out
you were a fan of it.

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:47.787
-We tweeted.
I tweeted to him a thank-you.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:50.723
I'm sure you all remember
looking for any sort of comfort

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:51.957
during the...
-Of course.

00:04:51.957 --> 00:04:53.993
-...the unpleasantness.
And that was something

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:55.694
we could do,
and it was a nice little time,

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:57.796
and we'd watch the show.

00:04:57.796 --> 00:04:59.298
So I --
yeah, I tweeted Nathan Fillion.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:00.733
I think my son
actually asked me,

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.902
"Can you please let them know
how much we love the show?"

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:04.670
-That's great.
-So I tweeted Nathan Fillion.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.105
He was very nice.
He wrote back.

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:08.907
He sent my son a care package
full of "Rookie" stuff,

00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:10.876
and then he said
when COVID clears up,

00:05:10.876 --> 00:05:12.377
we can visit the set sometime.

00:05:12.377 --> 00:05:15.481
-So you did, and not only
did you get to visit the set,

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.516
he gave you -- obviously,
you're very comfortable

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:19.852
in front of the camera --
a cameo, but also Jack.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:21.687
Was Jack at all hesitant?
-No.

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:23.689
Oh, my God, he was very excited.

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:25.624
He was very excited, and I was
trying to think what --

00:05:25.624 --> 00:05:28.528
and let me just also say,
Alexi Hawley, the showrunner,

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:30.162
and everybody there,
Nathan Fillion,

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:31.863
they were so nice.
And I can't imagine --

00:05:31.863 --> 00:05:33.365
I don't know what shows
you were into when you were like

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:35.802
12 or 13, but for me,
this would've been like

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:37.236
meeting the
Six Million Dollar Man,

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.805
hanging out with Lee Majors
or hanging out with Alan Alda

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:41.907
at the set of "MASH."
Like it was a dream for him.

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:43.442
He loved it.
-That's amazing.

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:45.010
And now we're gonna review
his acting performance

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.412
'cause I think he did
a pretty good job.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.747
-I think he was pretty good.
-I think he did a great job.

00:05:47.747 --> 00:05:49.114
Let's take a look
at the Tapper boys

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:50.849
here on an episode
of "The Rookie."

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:53.419
-Oh, that is a brilliant
costume, young man.

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:54.820
You too, slightly older man.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:58.124
I don't see many Philly fans
this side of the Rockies.

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:00.326
-You from there?
-Foxburg, actually.

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:01.594
Just moved here a few years ago.

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:04.297
-God, you look remarkably
like that fellow from CNN.

00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:06.365
What's his name?
-Jake Tapper.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:08.867
-No, that's not it.
-Yeah, it is.

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:10.303
Here, you guys want some candy?

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:12.371
-Thank the nice people.
-Thank you.

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:14.106
-Roger that.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:17.843
-I think he did a great job.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:17.843 --> 00:06:20.145
-Isn't he cute?
-He did great.

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:22.681
-He's such a --
he was so appreciative,

00:06:22.681 --> 00:06:24.216
and this is something
that you're able to do,

00:06:24.216 --> 00:06:25.717
I'm sure, because --
with your kids, too.

00:06:25.717 --> 00:06:28.321
But, like -- we were
driving back from the set,

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:30.756
and he actually was weeping
out of happiness,

00:06:30.756 --> 00:06:32.458
which I've never done
in my entire life.

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:33.659
I don't know
if any of you have --

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.194
-See, I'm not gonna
do it for my kids.

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:37.229
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:39.098
-It's a tough world.
-You gotta earn it.

00:06:39.098 --> 00:06:41.833
-It's a tough world.
-No free cameos.

00:06:41.833 --> 00:06:43.469
-If you want to be
on "The Rookie,"

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:45.170
you have to go through
the audition process

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:47.039
like every other kid.
-Everybody else.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:48.707
They were like,
"Will you tweet Nathan Fillion?"

00:06:48.707 --> 00:06:51.277
I'm like, "You get a Twitter
account, and you tweet him."

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:52.344
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.344 --> 00:06:53.512
"Dad, can I have money?

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:54.546
Can I have money
for a head shot?"

00:06:54.546 --> 00:06:55.881
You're like,
"Just use this picture

00:06:55.881 --> 00:06:58.717
of my dad as Mike Schmidt."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:00.452
-"Here's your grandpa
as Mike Schmidt.

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:02.154
That's your gift for the day."

00:07:02.154 --> 00:07:04.222
I have a million things
to ask you about,

00:07:04.222 --> 00:07:06.158
especially with the midterm
and the world at large.

00:07:06.158 --> 00:07:07.459
Will you stick around for it?
-Yes, of course.

00:07:07.459 --> 00:07:08.661
Thank you so much.
-All right.

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:10.363
We'll be right back with more
from Jake Tapper.

